1	/*
2	 * Input — Figma `Input`: text or number, optionally with a leading icon.
3	 *
4	 * ## behavior
5	 * - `InputVariant.text` (default) renders a bare `<input>` carrying its own
6	 *   chrome. Every other variant renders a wrapper `<div>` with the chrome
7	 *   (border, background, hover, `focus-within` ring) around a transparent
8	 *   `<input>`. `className` always lands on the chrome element; every other
9	 *   prop, and `ref`, always land on the `<input>`.
10	 * - Number variants use the mono role, pad 12px both sides, never shrink below
11	 *   one button height (a square) and set `inputMode="decimal"` (a consumer's
12	 *   own `inputMode` wins). By default they are FIXED width like the text
13	 *   variant — they fill their container or take a consumer width — and
14	 *   left-align their content, icon included. The opt-in `autoWidth` prop makes
15	 *   them hug the value instead and centre their content. The icon variants
16	 *   require an `icon` element.
17	 * - `format` (number variants only, default off) formats the value for
18	 *   display with `Intl.NumberFormat` — `true` or an options object, in `locale`
19	 *   (default: the runtime's). Format on blur: while the field is focused it
20	 *   shows the raw value, and it shows the formatted one otherwise. The raw
21	 *   value is a plain numeric string with "." as the decimal point. Users never
22	 *   type separators: grouping separators and whitespace are stripped from typed
23	 *   or pasted text, and the locale's decimal separator is read as the decimal
24	 *   point (the focused field shows that separator, the raw value does not).
25	 *   Text that is not a plain number is shown as typed, never reformatted.
26	 * - `onValueChange(value)` fires on every change with the raw string — the
27	 *   input's own value, or the stripped numeric string when `format` is on.
28	 *   `onChange` keeps native semantics and always still runs; with `format` on,
29	 *   its `event.target.value` is the displayed text, so read `onValueChange`.
30	 *   `value` / `defaultValue` are raw values in both modes.
31	 * - Number variants filter at ENTRY: only digits, one decimal separator and a
32	 *   leading minus (only when `min` is undefined or negative) can be typed; a
33	 *   keystroke or paste that would add nothing valid is blocked in
34	 *   `onBeforeInput`, and whatever slips through (partly valid paste,
35	 *   autofill, IME) is sanitised in the change handler before `onChange` /
36	 *   `onValueChange` run — so a paste of "12ab3.4.5" becomes "123.45". The
37	 *   decimal separator is the `locale`'s when `format` is on, else "."; the
38	 *   comma (or the locale's group separator) and whitespace are stripped as
39	 *   grouping. Consumer `onBeforeInput`, `onChange` and `onPaste` still run.
40	 * - `min` / `max` (number variants, numbers) never clamp. When the committed
41	 *   value is outside [min, max] the field shows the danger chrome and sets
42	 *   `aria-invalid`. Committed means the value on blur when uncontrolled (the
43	 *   `defaultValue` until then) and the current `value` when controlled.
44	 *   Empty or half-typed text ("-", ".") is never out of range.
45	 * - `hasError` paints the danger chrome and sets `aria-invalid` on the
46	 *   `<input>` (a consumer's own `aria-invalid` wins). It is authoritative:
47	 *   `true` forces the error, `false` suppresses the range-derived one, and
48	 *   only when it is omitted does the range decide.
49	 * - Clicking the wrapper's chrome (padding, icon) focuses the input.
50	 *
51	 * ## constraints
52	 * - ONLY `autoWidth` number variants HUG their value, through a hidden mirror
53	 *   span that shares a grid cell with the input. A native `<input>` sizes from
54	 *   its `size` attribute, never its value, so neither flex nor `width: auto`
55	 *   can make it hug; `field-sizing: content` is not in Safari or Firefox.
56	 *   Deleting the "invisible" span collapses an `autoWidth` field to the
57	 *   browser's ~20ch default. Without `autoWidth` there is no mirror span and
58	 *   the field must not hug: dashboards must not shift layout under typing.
59	 * - The mirror follows the text the field displays: the formatted value when
60	 *   `format` is on and the field is blurred, else the raw value — `value` when
61	 *   controlled, its own state (fed by `onChange`) when not. Writing
62	 *   `input.value` imperatively through the ref bypasses it — set `value`
63	 *   instead. The consumer's `onChange` always still runs.
64	 * - With `format` on the `<input>` is always controlled by this component (the
65	 *   displayed text differs from the raw value), so the DOM `value` is the
66	 *   display text and `defaultValue` is never passed down. Raw state stays
67	 *   `value` or the component's own state, never the display text, or blur
68	 *   would re-parse formatted text.
69	 * - Input filtering happens at entry, never as a validation error after the
70	 *   fact: an invalid character must not reach the DOM value, `onChange` or
71	 *   `onValueChange`. Range errors are DERIVED from the committed value on
72	 *   every render and never stored, and `hasError` always overrides them, so
73	 *   a consumer's own validation can't be fought by the automatic one. Never
74	 *   clamp `value`: the consumer decides what to do with an out-of-range number.
75	 * - `InputVariant.text` without `icon` stays a bare `<input>`: existing
76	 *   consumers style and measure that element directly, and wrapping it would
77	 *   move their `className` onto a different node.
78	 * - An icon variant without an `icon` element (null, false, "" included)
79	 *   throws. The props union is the real guard (`icon` is a `ReactElement`);
80	 *   the throw covers JavaScript consumers and runtime-computed variants —
81	 *   rendering a variant with an empty icon slot would silently misreport what
82	 *   was asked for (same rule as SliderVariant.custom).
83	 */
